1
00:00:00,090 --> 00:00:02,529
Open Shifts and find the Calendar section.

2
00:00:03,130 --> 00:00:07,239
Add Static Shift creates a dated coverage
window for selected units.

3
00:00:07,650 --> 00:00:12,229
Use this when you need a fixed commitment,
such as a community standby, a security

4
00:00:12,229 --> 00:00:14,510
detail or a search support period.

5
00:00:14,899 --> 00:00:17,239
The calendar in this exercise is November.

6
00:00:17,649 --> 00:00:20,379
Select Add Static Shift to open the form.

7
00:00:20,860 --> 00:00:24,579
Use a separate training unit while you
learn the save and update process.

8
00:00:24,810 --> 00:00:26,829
Give the shift a recognizable Name.

9
00:00:27,289 --> 00:00:30,189
The example uses Verified static coverage.

10
00:00:30,669 --> 00:00:34,970
Choose a light Color so the calendar’s
black text stays readable; the help

11
00:00:34,970 --> 00:00:36,549
beside the field calls this out.

12
00:00:37,100 --> 00:00:41,029
Add a Description explaining the work and
any coordination instructions.

13
00:00:41,459 --> 00:00:45,140
This fictional description also reminds
the editor to verify dates

14
00:00:45,170 --> 00:00:47,269
and retained units before updating.

15
00:00:47,699 --> 00:00:51,090
A clear title and description help people
recognize the commitment

16
00:00:51,119 --> 00:00:52,699
when they open it from the calendar.

17
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:57,319
Set Start Date and Time to November
twelfth at nine in the morning, and End

18
00:00:57,319 --> 00:00:59,280
Date and Time to five that afternoon.

19
00:00:59,760 --> 00:01:04,300
The start help describes when entities are
committed; the end help describes

20
00:01:04,329 --> 00:01:05,589
when they are available again.

21
00:01:06,070 --> 00:01:11,099
Under Units, select the fictional static
demo unit and confirm its selected tag.

22
00:01:11,529 --> 00:01:15,379
Check both dates and the chosen unit
before Create Static Shift.

23
00:01:15,740 --> 00:01:19,910
Dismiss the date picker so it does not
cover the unit selection or save control.

24
00:01:20,119 --> 00:01:24,739
Select Create Static Shift, then open the
matching event from the calendar.

25
00:01:25,199 --> 00:01:29,489
View Static Shift Day shows November
twelfth at nine, the shift title and

26
00:01:29,489 --> 00:01:31,760
description, and the selected unit.

27
00:01:32,119 --> 00:01:35,239
This is the saved result, not the
preparation form.

28
00:01:35,649 --> 00:01:40,170
The training unit has no configured
unit-role slots, so this example does not

29
00:01:40,170 --> 00:01:42,190
claim that any person has been assigned.

30
00:01:42,610 --> 00:01:46,690
Compare the saved day and unit with the
intended commitment before moving on.

31
00:01:46,880 --> 00:01:48,789
Select Edit Static Shift.

32
00:01:49,239 --> 00:01:53,099
The reopened dates retain nine in the
morning through five in the afternoon.

33
00:01:53,610 --> 00:01:57,400
Read the yellow warning before Update:
editing clears the shift’s

34
00:01:57,400 --> 00:02:00,880
assignments or signups and rebuilds them
from the new settings.

35
00:02:01,350 --> 00:02:05,170
In this version, the Units selection is
initially empty on Edit.

36
00:02:05,589 --> 00:02:08,560
Deliberately select every unit you intend
to retain.

37
00:02:09,019 --> 00:02:12,179
Coordinate the change before updating a
staffed operational

38
00:02:12,179 --> 00:02:16,810
shift; do not assume a saved date change
preserves existing signups.

39
00:02:16,959 --> 00:02:21,690
For this disposable exercise, move the
window to November thirteenth, ten in

40
00:02:21,690 --> 00:02:23,440
the morning through six in the evening.

41
00:02:23,909 --> 00:02:26,980
Reselect the same training unit and verify
its tag.

42
00:02:27,459 --> 00:02:30,180
Review the warning and select Update once.

43
00:02:30,569 --> 00:02:32,499
Open the saved calendar event again.

44
00:02:32,970 --> 00:02:36,220
Its day detail now shows November
thirteenth at ten,

45
00:02:36,349 --> 00:02:38,119
with the same unit still listed.

46
00:02:38,519 --> 00:02:42,419
That readback checks both the new date and
the unit submitted with the update.

47
00:02:42,630 --> 00:02:47,320
Reopen Edit to check the persisted start
and end values: ten A M and

48
00:02:47,320 --> 00:02:49,409
six P M on November thirteenth.

49
00:02:49,820 --> 00:02:53,410
The Units picker is empty again in this
editor, even though the saved

50
00:02:53,410 --> 00:02:55,120
day detail just showed the unit.

51
00:02:55,539 --> 00:02:58,289
Do not submit another update while merely
reviewing.

52
00:02:58,740 --> 00:03:01,709
Select Cancel to leave the stored shift
unchanged.

53
00:03:02,090 --> 00:03:05,830
Use the saved day detail to verify the
current unit commitment, and

54
00:03:05,830 --> 00:03:09,250
review the edit warning whenever a change
is actually required.

55
00:03:09,469 --> 00:03:14,679
Use this same create, verify and carefully
update process for search and rescue,

56
00:03:14,860 --> 00:03:19,410
security companies, wildland contractors
and industrial emergency teams.

57
00:03:19,790 --> 00:03:24,299
Community paramedicine, mental health
response, fire and E M S teams can

58
00:03:24,299 --> 00:03:26,099
plan fixed support windows as well.

59
00:03:26,519 --> 00:03:29,829
Choose a meaningful unit and coverage
period, keep calendar

60
00:03:29,829 --> 00:03:33,870
labels readable, and verify the saved
result after every change.

61
00:03:34,280 --> 00:03:38,099
This lesson covers a fixed unit
commitment; recurring rotations

62
00:03:38,099 --> 00:03:40,980
and personnel signup workflows have their
own walkthroughs.

